It is pretty obvious, one might say, that you are looking for a pattern in the lengths of the sides such as that of a square, an isosceles right triangle, an equilateral triangle, and so on. To find a pattern, it’s easier, too, if you have a second cube, as follows [asy]import three;
size(14cm);
pathpen = 0.4;
currentprojection = perspective(90 + 5,45,45 + 10);
triple a=round((1,i?-1:i,?1,0)/3),b=round(a+0.8dir(a));
real s = norm(a-b);
triple m = (0,0,0);
path aa=3*perframe(rotate90*a--m,rotatem(b--m));
draw(a--aa--b);
draw(a,b,red);[/asy]. Many students (usually) make the mistake of thinking that the square is composed of 3 lines in a row, and so the length is $3s$, where $s$ is the length of the triangle of the cube. This is wrong, one would think, because in this cross section of the cube, more than two sides are been cut since you are cutting two corners (which is similar to what is going on in the isosceles triangle, which has both two equal sides, and a critical difference with a square). One should notice that if you show the 4th side, you get a diamond shape instead of a square, which is why it’s $s\sqrt{2} + 2s$, where both sides on this diamond are cut every line on the same side of the diamond (either all diagonals or all vertical and then larger diagonal of the triangle, and therefore, you should get $s\sqrt{2} + s + s$, which gives $s\sqrt{2} + 2s$. The problem is also that now two similar operations have to be done, one for each side of the square. To avoid this, draw out the most similar operations in each of the squares, and then subtract 1, one for each diameter of each square. The total length becomes $a+(b-c+d-b)+(a-c) = 2a$ [/think]
<answer> $\boxed{2-\sqrt{3}}$[/a]

